1. What is a server?
    A server is a computer (hardware and/or software) that provides services to other computers over a network.
    Unlike a personal computer, it runs 24/7 and is designed to handle multiple simultaneous requests.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. What is the role of the domain name?
    The domain name (foobar.com) is a human-readable alias for an IP address.
    Instead of typing 8.8.8.8, users type www.foobar.com. The DNS system handles the translation.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. What type of DNS record is www in www.foobar.com?
    It's an A record. An A record maps a hostname directly to an IPv4 address, here www.foobar → 8.8.8.8.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. What is the role of the web server (Nginx)?
    NginX receives incoming HTTP/HTTPS requests.
    It serves static files (images, CSS, JS) directly, and forwards dynamic requests to the application server.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. What is the role of the application server?
    It executes the application's business logic (the code).
    It processes dynamic requests, interacts with the database, and returns the result to Nginx, which sends it back to the user.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. What is the role of the database (MySQL)?
    It stores and manages persistent data - users, content, orders, etc. The application server queries it via SQL to read or write data.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. What does the server use to communicate with the user's computer?
    The HTTP/HTTPS protocol, transported over TCP/IP.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

8. SPOF (Single Point of Failure)
    There is only one server. If it goes down, the entire website is unavailable - no redundancy, no failover.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

9. Downtime during maintenance 
    Deploying new code often requires restarting Nginx or the app server. During that restart, the site is unreachable.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

10. Cannot scale with high traffic
    One server has limited CPU and RAM. If traffic spikes, it gets overwhelmed. There is no way to add more servers to distribute the load.